S={
"sufentanil":(["Sufentanil is among the strongest opioids used in medicine, so its character in practice is almost entirely clinical: a fast, deep, heavy sedation in an operating room or intensive care unit, with the patient breathing only because a machine or anesthetist is helping.","Patients given it during surgery rarely remember it. Those given the sublingual tablet (Dsuvia) in supervised settings described rapid, heavy drowsiness and a floating detachment from pain, with nausea common afterward.","Its strength means there is almost no gap between pain relief and breathing failure outside a monitored setting."],
 ["IV: about one minute; sublingual: around 15 to 30 minutes.","Almost no come-up: drowsiness falls quickly.","Deep sedation; chest-wall stiffness can occur with fast IV use.","Sedation for under an hour after a single IV bolus; longer with infusions.","Wears off quickly once stopped, though it builds up in fat with long infusions.","Grogginess, nausea, itching.","Usually little lingering effect next day."]),
"alfentanil":(["Alfentanil is a very short-acting fentanyl relative used for brief procedures. Its character is a quick, sharp wave of sedation and pain relief that ebbs within minutes.","Patients describe a sudden heaviness, a warm rush in the chest, and then very little memory of the procedure.","Because it acts and fades fast, it is used for short painful moments, not lasting relief."],
 ["IV: under one minute.","Essentially immediate.","Peaks within about 1 to 2 minutes.","Lasts only about 5 to 10 minutes after one dose.","Fades fast.","Brief nausea or dizziness.","No next-day effect from the drug itself."]),
"remifentanil":(["Remifentanil is broken down by enzymes in the blood within minutes, so it acts like an on-off switch. Its character is intense, controllable pain relief that vanishes almost as soon as the infusion stops.","Patients waking after surgery with remifentanil may suddenly feel pain because nothing lingers; it is also used in labor with patient-controlled pumps under close monitoring.","Its abrupt offset can bring a sharp rebound of pain and, in some people, increased pain sensitivity afterward."],
 ["IV: about one minute.","Immediate.","Peak within minutes of starting or changing an infusion.","Steady only while the infusion runs.","Gone within about 5 to 10 minutes of stopping.","Rebound pain, shivering, nausea.","No drug left by next day."]),
"butorphanol":(["Butorphanol is a mixed agonist-antagonist: it activates kappa receptors more strongly than most opioids. Its character is heavy drowsiness and a floating, sometimes unreal feeling, rather than the warm contentment of morphine.","The nasal spray was popular for migraines. Many users reported dizziness, a 'spaced-out' or dissociated state, and sometimes anxiety or strange thoughts from the kappa effects.","It has a ceiling on breathing depression, but sedation still adds dangerously to alcohol and benzodiazepines, and in people dependent on other opioids it can trigger withdrawal."],
 ["Nasal spray: within about 15 minutes; injection: a few minutes.","Drowsiness and lightheadedness rise fast.","Peak around 30 to 60 minutes: heavy sleepiness, dizziness, sometimes a dreamy unreality.","Sedation for a few hours.","Fades over about 3 to 5 hours.","Headache, nausea, lingering fog.","Some feel washed-out next day."]),
"nalbuphine":(["Nalbuphine (Nubain) is a kappa agonist and mu partial antagonist given by injection. Its character is sedation with relatively mild euphoria; many patients feel sleepy and calm rather than high.","It was used by some bodybuilders in the 1990s and 2000s, leading to dependence in that group.","It can reverse some effects of other opioids and is sometimes used to relieve opioid-induced itching."],
 ["Injection: about 2 to 15 minutes.","Quick drowsiness.","Peak around 30 minutes: sleepiness and pain relief.","Lasts about 3 to 6 hours.","Gentle fade.","Grogginess, sweating, sometimes dizziness.","Little next-day effect."]),
"pentazocine":(["Pentazocine (Talwin) has strong kappa effects, so it can feel unlike other opioids: some people feel pleasant drowsiness, others feel anxious, confused, or experience hallucinations and nightmares.","In the 1970s, crushed Talwin tablets mixed with an antihistamine ('T's and Blues') were injected; the US added naloxone to the tablets in 1983 (Talwin NX) to discourage this.","Injection damage from pentazocine was notorious: hard, scarred skin and muscle at injection sites."],
 ["Oral: about 15 to 30 minutes; injection: a few minutes.","Drowsiness and sometimes unease.","Peak around 1 to 2 hours, mixed sedation and odd thoughts.","Sedation for a few hours.","Fades over about 3 to 4 hours.","Possible nightmares, sweating, dizziness.","Some feel unsettled next day."]),
"dextromoramide":(["Dextromoramide (Palfium) was known for a fast, strong, short effect, described as a brief, intense opioid wave lasting a couple of hours.","Its short duration led to frequent redosing among people misusing it in Europe in the 1970s and 1980s, and it was considered highly addictive.","It is now withdrawn in most countries."],
 ["Oral or sublingual: about 10 to 20 minutes.","Rapid warmth and drowsiness.","Peak within about an hour, quite strong.","Short plateau.","Fades over 2 to 3 hours.","Craving and restlessness as it wears off.","Next-day low mood if used heavily."]),
"dipipanone":(["Dipipanone is a methadone-related opioid that was combined with the anti-sickness drug cyclizine (Diconal) in the UK. The combination had a dreamy, heavy character with an anticholinergic edge, including dry mouth and odd, hazy thoughts.","Diconal became notorious in the 1970s and 1980s when tablets were crushed and injected, causing severe vein and tissue damage.","It is now rarely prescribed."],
 ["Oral: about 30 minutes.","Drowsy, dry-mouthed warmth.","Peak 1 to 2 hours with sedation and haze.","A few hours of sedation.","Fades over 4 to 6 hours.","Dry mouth, confusion, constipation.","Grogginess next day."]),
"piritramide":(["Piritramide (Dipidolor) is used in Germany and neighboring countries after surgery, given by injection or pump. Its character is strong sedation with relatively little nausea compared with morphine.","Patients describe sleepiness and calm detachment; euphoria is moderate.","It is mainly a hospital drug."],
 ["Injection: a few minutes (IV) to 15 minutes (IM).","Drowsiness builds fast.","Peak within about 15 to 30 minutes.","Lasts about 4 to 6 hours.","Gentle fade.","Grogginess.","Little next-day effect."]),
"ethylmorphine":(["Ethylmorphine is a cousin of codeine and partly turns into morphine in the liver. Its character is mild: a cough-quieting calm with light drowsiness and a faint warm glow.","Like codeine, how strongly it acts depends partly on liver enzymes, so some people feel almost nothing and others feel more.","It is mostly found in cough medicines in a few countries."],
 ["Oral: about 30 to 60 minutes.","Mild warmth.","Peak 1 to 2 hours, mild.","A few hours of calm.","Fades over 4 to 6 hours.","Constipation, itch.","Little next-day effect."]),
"nicomorphine":(["Nicomorphine (Vilan) is a morphine ester that converts to morphine in the body, much as heroin does. Its character is similar to heroin or morphine: a warm, heavy contentment with a faster start than plain morphine when injected.","It was used for pain after surgery and in childbirth in parts of Europe.","It is rare today."],
 ["Injection: a few minutes.","Warm rush and heaviness.","Peak within 30 minutes.","Lasts a few hours.","Fades over 3 to 5 hours.","Nausea, itch.","Grogginess."]),
"dihydromorphine":(["Dihydromorphine (Paramorfan) is close to morphine in structure and feel, with a slightly faster onset and shorter action. Its character is the familiar morphine-like warmth, sedation, and itch.","It is used rarely, mainly in parts of Europe and Japan.","Little modern research exists."],
 ["Injection: a few minutes; oral: about 30 minutes.","Warmth and heaviness.","Peak about 30 to 60 minutes.","A few hours.","Fades over about 4 hours.","Itch, nausea.","Grogginess."]),
"anileridine":(["Anileridine (Leritine) is a meperidine relative used in the 1950s to 1970s. Its character was described as similar to meperidine: quick, lighter sedation with less constipation.","It was withdrawn in the US and Canada.","Modern firsthand reports are almost nonexistent."],
 ["Oral or injection: about 15 minutes.","Quick drowsiness.","Peak within about an hour.","Short plateau.","Fades over 2 to 3 hours.","Dizziness.","Little next-day effect."]),
"alphaprodine":(["Alphaprodine (Nisentil) was a short-acting opioid once used in labor and dentistry. Its character was a fast, short sedation.","It was associated with breathing problems in infants and was withdrawn in the US.","Firsthand reports are historical."],
 ["Injection: about 1 to 2 minutes.","Immediate drowsiness.","Peak within 10 minutes.","Short plateau.","Gone in 1 to 2 hours.","Dizziness.","None."]),
"benzhydrocodone":(["Benzhydrocodone (in Apadaz, with acetaminophen) is a prodrug that the gut turns into hydrocodone. Its character when swallowed is close to immediate-release hydrocodone: a warm, mildly euphoric pain relief with itch.","It was designed so that snorting or injecting releases little hydrocodone, but swallowing too much still causes overdose, and the acetaminophen can injure the liver.","It is used for short-term acute pain."],
 ["Oral: about 30 to 60 minutes (slightly slower than plain hydrocodone).","Gentle warmth.","Peak around 1 to 2 hours.","A few hours of relief.","Fades over 4 to 6 hours.","Constipation, itch, nausea.","Little next-day effect."]),
"meptazinol":(["Meptazinol (Meptid) is a partial opioid with some cholinergic effects. Its character is mild pain relief with less sedation and euphoria than morphine, and more nausea.","It was used in the UK, including in labor.","Misuse was uncommon."],
 ["Oral: about 30 minutes; injection: faster.","Mild drowsiness.","Peak around 1 to 2 hours.","A few hours.","Fades over 3 to 5 hours.","Nausea, dizziness.","Little next-day effect."]),
"dezocine":(["Dezocine is a mixed agonist-antagonist that was withdrawn in the US but became one of the most-used hospital painkillers in China. Its character is sedation and pain relief with mild euphoria.","Because of its partial action, it can cause withdrawal in people dependent on full opioids.","Firsthand recreational reports are rare."],
 ["Injection: about 15 minutes.","Drowsiness.","Peak 30 to 60 minutes.","A few hours.","Fades over 3 to 6 hours.","Dizziness, nausea.","Little next-day effect."]),
"nitazenes":(["Nitazenes (isotonitazene, metonitazene, protonitazene, N-pyrrolidino etonitazene and others) are benzimidazole opioids developed in the 1950s and never approved. Their character is like a very strong, fast fentanyl-like hit: a heavy wave of warmth, then sudden, deep sedation.","Many people who die from nitazenes did not know they were taking them, because they are hidden in fake oxycodone, fake benzodiazepine tablets, and heroin.","Some nitazenes are far stronger than fentanyl, so the step from a pleasant effect to stopped breathing is tiny, and more than one naloxone dose may be needed."],
 ["Smoked or injected: seconds; swallowed: about 15 to 45 minutes.","Very fast, overwhelming heaviness.","Peak deep sedation and nodding; breathing can slow sharply.","Plateau varies; some last longer than fentanyl.","Fade is unpredictable and overdose can return after naloxone wears off.","Exhaustion, nausea.","Withdrawal may begin."]),
"u-47700":(["U-47700 ('U4', 'pink') was designed by Upjohn in the 1970s and sold online from about 2015. Users described a shorter, sharper morphine-like effect with strong euphoria at first but quick tolerance and short duration.","Its short effect drove repeated use, and it was linked to many deaths before being placed in Schedule I in 2016.","It was found in fake tablets and mixed with fentanyl."],
 ["Snorted or swallowed: about 5 to 30 minutes.","Fast warmth and euphoria.","Peak within an hour.","Short plateau.","Fades in 3 to 4 hours.","Craving to redose.","Withdrawal-like low."]),
"ah-7921":(["AH-7921 was developed by Allen and Hanburys in the 1970s and sold online around 2012 to 2014. Users described a morphine-like effect with sedation and itch.","It was associated with deaths in Europe and placed under international control.","Little reliable information exists."],
 ["Swallowed: about 30 minutes.","Warmth builds.","Peak about 1 to 2 hours.","A few hours.","Fades over about 4 to 6 hours.","Nausea, itch.","Grogginess."]),
"mt-45":(["MT-45 is a piperazine opioid developed in Japan in the 1970s. Users described an opioid effect with some dissociation.","It was linked to unusual harms: hearing loss, hair loss, and skin and nail problems, as well as deaths in Sweden around 2013 and 2014.","It was placed under international control."],
 ["Swallowed or snorted: about 15 to 45 minutes.","Warmth and slight dissociation.","Peak 1 to 2 hours.","A few hours.","Fades over about 4 to 6 hours.","Nausea; reported hearing problems.","Tiredness."]),
"carfentanil":(["Carfentanil was designed to sedate elephants and other large animals. It is so potent that amounts invisible to the eye can stop a person's breathing.","There is no meaningful recreational 'character' to describe safely: people who encounter it are often caught off guard by sudden unconsciousness within moments.","It appeared in heroin and fake pills in Ohio in 2016 and in later waves. Multiple naloxone doses and emergency care are often needed."],
 ["Seconds to minutes by any route.","None to speak of: collapse can come first.","Profound sedation and breathing failure.","Long-acting; can outlast naloxone.","Overdose can return after naloxone wears off.","Severe exhaustion, possible brain injury from low oxygen.","Medical follow-up needed."]),
"temazepam":(["Temazepam (Restoril, Normison) is a sleep-focused benzodiazepine with an intermediate duration. Its character is a heavy, comfortable drowsiness that comes on within about 30 to 60 minutes.","In the UK in the 1990s, liquid-filled capsules ('jellies') were injected, causing severe vein damage, gangrene, and amputations; the gel capsules were later withdrawn.","Next-morning grogginess and memory gaps are common at higher amounts."],
 ["Swallowed: about 30 to 60 minutes.","Heaviness and yawning.","Peak 1 to 2 hours: deep sleepiness.","Sleep for several hours.","Fades over about 6 to 10 hours.","Grogginess.","Morning fog."]),
"triazolam":(["Triazolam (Halcion) is a very short-acting, strong sleeping pill. Its character is a quick plunge into sleepiness and, often, amnesia: people may do and say things they later cannot remember.","It has been linked to reports of confusion, unusual behavior, and next-day anxiety; it was banned or restricted in the UK in 1991.","Rebound insomnia and anxiety can follow when it wears off."],
 ["Swallowed: about 15 to 30 minutes.","Fast drowsiness.","Peak within about 1 to 2 hours, strong amnesia.","A few hours.","Fades in about 4 to 6 hours.","Early-morning wakening, anxiety.","Rebound anxiety."]),
"oxazepam":(["Oxazepam (Serax, Serepax) is slow to start and mild. Its character is gentle calm and loosening without a sudden 'hit', which made it less popular for misuse.","It is processed without the liver enzymes many other benzodiazepines need, so it is used in older adults and people with liver disease, and in alcohol withdrawal.","Effects feel mellow and even."],
 ["Swallowed: about 45 to 90 minutes.","Slow, soft relaxation.","Peak around 2 to 3 hours.","Several hours of calm.","Fades over 6 to 10 hours.","Mild sleepiness.","Little next-day effect."]),
"clorazepate":(["Clorazepate (Tranxene) is a prodrug that turns into desmethyldiazepam, the same long-lasting metabolite as diazepam. Its character is a steady, long, physical calm.","It is used for anxiety, seizures, and alcohol withdrawal.","Its effects linger for days."],
 ["Swallowed: about 30 to 60 minutes.","Muscle and mental relaxation.","Peak around 1 to 2 hours.","Long plateau.","Fades over many hours to days.","Drowsiness.","Still active."]),
"flurazepam":(["Flurazepam (Dalmane) is a sleeping pill with a very long-lasting metabolite. Its character is a smooth slide into sleep, followed by next-day drowsiness that can build over nights.","In older adults, this lingering effect raises the risk of falls and confusion.","It is now rarely prescribed."],
 ["Swallowed: about 15 to 45 minutes.","Sleepiness.","Peak 1 to 2 hours.","Sleep.","Metabolite lasts for days.","Morning grogginess.","Hangover that accumulates."]),
"estazolam":(["Estazolam (ProSom) is an intermediate-acting sleeping pill. Its character is steady drowsiness and sleep through the night.","Next-day sleepiness is possible.","It is a typical, less-misused hypnotic."],
 ["Swallowed: about 30 to 60 minutes.","Drowsiness.","Peak about 1 to 2 hours.","Sleep.","Fades over 8 to 12 hours.","Grogginess.","Mild morning fog."]),
"quazepam":(["Quazepam (Doral) is a long-acting sleeping pill that acts more selectively on sleep-related receptors. Its character is sleepiness with long-lasting effects.","Its metabolites linger for days.","It is uncommon."],
 ["Swallowed: about 30 to 60 minutes.","Drowsiness.","Peak about 2 hours.","Sleep.","Very long fade.","Grogginess.","Still active."]),
"midazolam":(["Midazolam (Versed) is the main benzodiazepine used before and during procedures. Its character is fast, strong sedation and amnesia: patients often have no memory of the procedure, though they may seem awake.","It is also used as a nasal spray (Nayzilam) and an injection for seizure emergencies.","Combined with opioids, it can stop breathing; it is used with monitoring."],
 ["IV: 1 to 5 minutes; nasal: about 5 to 10 minutes.","Very quick heaviness.","Peak within minutes.","Short: about 1 to 2 hours.","Fades fast.","Amnesia, grogginess.","Usually no next-day effect."]),
"remimazolam":(["Remimazolam (Byfavo) is an ultra-short benzodiazepine broken down in the blood, approved in 2020 for procedural sedation. Its character is a quick, controllable sedation that wears off within minutes.","It is only used in monitored hospital settings.","There is no recreational use pattern."],
 ["IV: about 1 to 2 minutes.","Immediate.","Peak within minutes.","Only while given.","Wears off in about 10 to 20 minutes.","Brief grogginess.","None."]),
"flunitrazepam":(["Flunitrazepam (Rohypnol) is a strong, fast sleeping drug. Its character is heavy sedation and profound amnesia, which is why it became notorious as a drug used in sexual assault.","The manufacturer changed tablets so they turn drinks blue. It is not approved in the US.","Combined with alcohol, it causes blackouts and dangerous breathing problems."],
 ["Swallowed: about 15 to 30 minutes.","Heavy drowsiness.","Peak 1 to 2 hours.","Many hours of sedation.","Fades over 8 to 12 hours.","Amnesia.","Grogginess."]),
"nitrazepam":(["Nitrazepam (Mogadon) is a long-acting sleeping pill, popular in the UK in the 1970s. Its character is smooth sleep with next-day sluggishness.","It is still used for some childhood epilepsies.","It lingers long in older adults."],
 ["Swallowed: about 30 to 60 minutes.","Drowsiness.","Peak about 2 hours.","Sleep.","Long fade.","Grogginess.","Hangover."]),
"bromazepam":(["Bromazepam (Lexotan, Lexomil) is an anxiety benzodiazepine common in Europe and Latin America. Its character is a calm with muscle relaxation and less sleepiness than sleeping pills.","It is widely misused in some countries.","It has an intermediate duration."],
 ["Swallowed: about 30 to 60 minutes.","Calm.","Peak 1 to 2 hours.","Several hours.","Fades over 8 to 12 hours.","Drowsiness.","Mild fog."]),
"clobazam":(["Clobazam (Onfi, Frisium) is a 1,5-benzodiazepine used mainly for epilepsy, including Lennox-Gastaut syndrome. Its character is relatively mild sedation compared with other benzodiazepines.","It is not commonly misused.","Its long metabolite builds up."],
 ["Swallowed: about 30 to 60 minutes.","Gentle calm.","Peak 1 to 4 hours.","Long.","Fades over many hours.","Drowsiness.","Still active."]),
"lormetazepam":(["Lormetazepam (Noctamid) is a European sleeping pill. Its character is quick, smooth sleep.","It is also used in an injectable form in Germany.","Its duration is intermediate."],
 ["Swallowed: about 30 minutes.","Drowsiness.","Peak 1 to 2 hours.","Sleep.","Fades over about 8 to 10 hours.","Grogginess.","Mild fog."]),
"prazepam":(["Prazepam (Centrax) is a prodrug for desmethyldiazepam, so it is slow and long-lasting. Its character is gradual, steady calm.","It is rarely used now.","It has low appeal for misuse because of its slow start."],
 ["Swallowed: about 1 to 2 hours.","Slow relaxation.","Peak several hours later.","Long.","Days.","Drowsiness.","Still active."]),
"etizolam":(["Etizolam is a thienodiazepine prescribed in Japan, India, and Italy, and sold as a designer drug elsewhere. Its character is quick calm with mild euphoria and less heavy sleepiness than alprazolam.","In Scotland, fake 'Valium' made with etizolam caused many deaths alongside opioids around 2017 to 2019.","Tolerance and dependence build quickly."],
 ["Swallowed: about 15 to 30 minutes.","Calm.","Peak 1 to 2 hours.","A few hours.","Fades over 6 to 8 hours.","Drowsiness.","Mild fog."]),
}
